1. Everything is reserved unless stated otherwise
--------------------------------------------------

Unless a section below says otherwise, no permission is granted to copy,
modify, redistribute, sublicense, sell, or otherwise use any part of this
repository. This covers, without limitation:

  - game source code and game data (game/)
  - art, sprites, atlases, and any generated or rendered images
    (game/assets/, art-source/)
  - design specifications that describe how a character, weapon, armor,
    outfit, or skin is built, including the part specifications and baking
    values under tools/blender/ (for example tools/blender/BakeSpec.ts)
  - documentation (docs/)
  - tests and tooling not covered by section 2

Viewing and forking the repository through GitHub's own functionality is
permitted by GitHub's Terms of Service. Nothing here grants more than that.

2. Exception — Blender Python scripts
--------------------------------------

The files matching

    tools/blender/**/*.py

call the Blender Python API. Blender's license requires scripts that use
that API to be shared under a GPL-compatible license when they are
published. Those files, and only those files, are licensed under the GNU
General Public License, version 3 or (at your option) any later version
(SPDX: GPL-3.0-or-later). The full text is in

    LICENSES/GPL-3.0-or-later.txt

    Copyright (c) 2026 dev-HongCheol

    These programs are free software: you can redistribute them and/or
    modify them under the terms of the GNU General Public License as
    published by the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the
    License, or (at your option) any later version.

    These programs are distributed in the hope that they will be useful,
    but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
    MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the GNU
    General Public License for more details.

The exception stops at those files. It does not reach:

  - the TypeScript files in the same directory (tools/blender/**/*.ts).
    They start Blender as a separate process from the command line and do
    not call its API; they fall under section 1.
  - the JSON specifications passed to the scripts, and the images, sprites,
    and models produced by running them. They are inputs and outputs, not
    the scripts, and fall under section 1.

3. Third-party tools and material
----------------------------------

Tools, engines, models, and services used to build the game remain under
their own terms. The terms that were checked, with the date each was read,
are recorded in docs/development/spec/ops-licensing.md.

